    "bounding box" : [ 227.24, 646.493, 257.717, 653.707 ],
    "font" : "ZapfDingbats",
    "font size" : 7.492,
    "text color" : "[0.7140001654624939, 0.6630045175552368, 0.650995671749115]",
    "content" : "HHHHH"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 31,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 227.24, 617.838, 319.108, 645.864 ],
    "font" : "BentonModernTwo-Italic",
    "font size" : 5.994,
    "text color" : "[0.13725490868091583, 0.12156862765550613, 0.125490203499794]",
    "content" : "Dir: DanFogelman, 117mins,starring: OscarIsaac,Olivia Wilde,Annette Bening,MandyPatinkin, OliviaCooke, Laia Costa,AntonioBanderas"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 38,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 331.733, 567.757, 427.295, 625.33 ],
    "font" : "BentonModernTwo-Roman",
    "font size" : 6.968,
    "text color" : "[0.13725490868091583, 0.12156862765550613, 0.125490203499794]",
    "content" : "Just as Will courted Abby, proud and hard-working farm worker Javier (Sergio Peris-Mencheta) sets out to win the hand of theshyandcharmingIsabel(Laia Costa). The couple’s idyllic life is disturbed by Javier’s boss, Vincent (AntonioBanderas)."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 7,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 123.813, 546.429, 219.328, 618.23 ],
    "font" : "BentonModernTwo-Roman",
    "font size" : 6.968,
    "text color" : "[0.13725490868091583, 0.12156862765550613, 0.125490203499794]",
    "content" : "They’re an attractive couple with a shared sense of mischief. We are aware from the outset that Colette is cleverer and more talented than her husband, who blithely fritters away the money they earn on gambling, race horses and women. It is also clear that he is the one who (whatever his motives)kick-startedher career."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 32,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 227.24, 574.912, 323.617, 611.123 ],
    "font" : "BentonModernTwo-Roman",
    "font size" : 6.968,
    "text color" : "[0.13725490868091583, 0.12156862765550613, 0.125490203499794]",
    "content" : "Life is the “ultimate unreliable narrator”.Thatisoneofthegreeting card-style nuggets of wisdom shared in Dan Fogelman’s trite andmanipulativesaga."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 33,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 227.24, 539.329, 323.666, 575.518 ],
    "font" : "BentonModernTwo-Roman",
    "font size" : 6.968,
    "text color" : "[0.13725490868091583, 0.12156862765550613, 0.125490203499794]",
    "content" : "Fogelman is one of many filmmakers in recent years to explore the butterﬂy effect: a small action byonecharacterhasaseismicripple effectonthelivesofothers."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 39,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 331.733, 503.689, 427.3, 568.37 ],
    "font" : "BentonModernTwo-Roman",
    "font size" : 6.968,
    "text color" : "[0.13725490868091583, 0.12156862765550613, 0.125490203499794]",
    "content" : "Vincent is a grizzled and menacing ﬁgure but one prey to selfpity, too. Like most of the others here, he has been bruised emotionally by horriﬁc childhood experiences. We can’t tell whether he wants to help Javier or steal Isabel. At least Banderas brings somecharisma tohis role."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 8,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 123.813, 532.194, 219.328, 547.042 ],
    "font" : "BentonModernTwo-Roman",
    "font size" : 6.968,
    "text color" : "[0.13725490868091583, 0.12156862765550613, 0.125490203499794]",
    "content" : "The film-makers use waltz music and montages as we gallop"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 34,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 227.24, 489.489, 323.647, 539.935 ],
    "font" : "BentonModernTwo-Roman",
    "font size" : 6.968,
    "text color" : "[0.13725490868091583, 0.12156862765550613, 0.125490203499794]",
    "content" : "OscarIsaacplaysWill,adreamy New Yorker who, as a young student,waitsfortheperfectmoment to ask out Abby (Olivia Wilde). They are so madly in love that it is inevitable that fate (or Fogelman) willplayafewtricksonthem."
  }, {
    "type" : "heading",
    "pdfua_tag" : "H3",
    "id" : 43,
    "level" : "Subtitle",
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 124.412, 480.474, 215.873, 521.768 ],
    "heading level" : 3,
    "font" : "StRyde-Bold",
    "font size" : 10.489,
    "text color" : "[0.13725490868091583, 0.12156862765550613, 0.125490203499794]",
    "content" : "WillyandColette areanattractive couplewithashared senseofmischief"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 40,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 331.733, 453.871, 427.285, 504.296 ],
    "font" : "BentonModernTwo-Roman",
    "font size" : 6.968,
    "text color" : "[0.13725490868091583, 0.12156862765550613, 0.125490203499794]",
    "content" : "In an otherwise bland affair, English actress Olivia Cooke shows some welcome rebellious attitude as a young woman scarred emotionally by events in her earliest infancy and ready to hit out atanyonewhoteasesher."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 35,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 227.24, 439.664, 323.666, 490.096 ],
    "font" : "BentonModernTwo-Roman",
    "font size" : 6.968,
    "text color" : "[0.13725490868091583, 0.12156862765550613, 0.125490203499794]",
    "content" : "Some of the dialogue is stilted and self-conscious. Abby can’t resist talking about her thesis at length. By exploring the role of unreliable narrators in literature, she thinks she has discovered the secretoflifeitself."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 9,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 123.813, 425.416, 219.342, 475.861 ],
    "font" : "BentonModernTwo-Roman",
    "font size" : 6.968,
    "text color" : "[0.13725490868091583, 0.12156862765550613, 0.125490203499794]",
    "content" : "throughthe1890sandearly1900s. Midway through, the film turns brieﬂyintoafarce as bothColette and Willy become enraptured by Georgie Raoul-Duval (Eleanor Tomlinson), a bisexual “wayward debutantefromLouisiana”."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 41,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 331.733, 375.569, 427.315, 454.477 ],
    "font" : "BentonModernTwo-Roman",
    "font size" : 6.968,
    "text color" : "[0.13725490868091583, 0.12156862765550613, 0.125490203499794]",
    "content" : "The film touches again and againonthemiseryinitsprotagonists’ lives but never steps too far into the darkness. For all the talk aboutunreliablenarrators,wecan almost always guess how events will unfold. Coincidences abound and you are always so aware that Fogelman is pulling the strings that it is impossible to lose yourselfinhis story. THE INDEPENDENT"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 36,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 227.24, 375.611, 323.66, 440.277 ],
    "font" : "BentonModernTwo-Roman",
    "font size" : 6.968,
    "text color" : "[0.13725490868091583, 0.12156862765550613, 0.125490203499794]",
    "content" : "Fogelman tells the story out of sequence. It is full of flashbacks and leaps into the future. Will is showninsessionswithatherapist (Annette Bening), failing to pull himself together. In one scene, the sweet-natured ﬁgure from earlier has turned into a raging, drunken, disturbedhobo."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 10,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 123.813, 332.886, 219.3, 426.029 ],
    "font" : "BentonModernTwo-Roman",
    "font size" : 6.968,
    "text color" : "[0.13725490868091583, 0.12156862765550613, 0.125490203499794]",
    "content" : "Colette is far too strong a character to fall prey to romantic despair or to allow her spendthrift and philandering husband to upset her. Yet her ability to cope means the film is often short on tension. Whatever the situation, she makes the best of it. None of her relationships, whether with Willy or, later, with the androgynousaristocrat,Mathilde“Missy” de Morny (Denise Gough), are especiallyturbulent."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 44,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 19.487, 340.042, 114.979, 411.836 ],
    "font" : "BentonModernTwo-Roman",
    "font size" : 6.968,
    "text color" : "[0.13725490868091583, 0.12156862765550613, 0.125490203499794]",
    "content" : "Keira Knightley plays aspiring author Colette as a passionate and fiercely independent figure. She is looking to establish an artistic identity of her own at a time when patriarchal wisdom has it that“ladywritersdon’tsell”.First seenasa19-year-oldin1892,sheis a young woman without a dowry oranyobviousprospects."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 23,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 383.86, 325.971, 423.399, 368.892 ],
    "font" : "StRyde-Bold",
    "font size" : 6.368,
    "text color" : "[0.13725490868091583, 0.12156862765550613, 0.125490203499794]",
    "content" : "Star-cross’d? LaiaCostaand SergioPerisMencheta asJavier andIsabel"
  }, {
    "type" : "image",
    "pdfua_tag" : "Figure",
    "id" : 46,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 227.688, 270.108, 379.632, 367.092 ],
    "alt_source" : "missing",
    "source" : "20_pg33_pg1_images/imageFile2.png"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 45,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 19.487, 268.84, 115.011, 340.648 ],
    "font" : "BentonModernTwo-Roman",
    "font size" : 6.968,
    "text color" : "[0.13725490868091583, 0.12156862765550613, 0.125490203499794]",
    "content" : "Colette may seem demure but that doesn’t stop her from having sex in the barn with the much older Henry Gauthier-Villars (known as “Willy”), a city slicker who served in the army with her father. Willy (Dominic West), who eventually becomes her husband, lures her from home to live in a Paris “heaving with artists and"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 11,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 123.813, 268.805, 219.283, 333.492 ],
    "font" : "BentonModernTwo-Roman",
    "font size" : 6.968,
    "text color" : "[0.13725490868091583, 0.12156862765550613, 0.125490203499794]",
    "content" : "The ﬁlm deals with a relatively small part of its subject’s story, longbefore she wrote Gigi.Thisis the author in her formative years, and as a portrait of an artist as a young woman, the ﬁlm is intriguing,Knightleyexcellinginthetitle role. But, as drama, it feels a little under-charged. THE INDEPENDENT"
